      "title": "Credit reports and the applicant’s rights",
      "narration": "When Rosa applies, the insurer may order an investigative consumer report about her character, reputation and lifestyle. The Fair Credit Reporting Act requires that she be told, within three days of the request, that such a report may be made, and that she may ask about its nature and scope. If the insurer takes adverse action based on a report, such as declining or rating her, it must tell her and identify the reporting agency so she can obtain the report and dispute errors. Producers who collect applications must know these notices exist and never discourage an applicant from exercising them.",
